CLEAR(1)                      Funny CLI Manual                      CLEAR(1)

NAME
    clear — Clear the terminal screen, because sometimes the only
            solution to terminal chaos is pretending it never happened.

SYNOPSIS
    clear
    cls

DESCRIPTION
    The 'clear' command clears the current terminal screen.

    It detects the operating system and uses the appropriate system
    command:

        Windows      → cls
        Everything else → clear

    Because terminals may differ, but the desire to make the mess
    disappear is universal.

ALIASES
    cls
        An alternate name for 'clear', provided for users who grew up
        on Windows or simply enjoy typing fewer syllables.

BEHAVIOR
    The command invokes the operating system's screen-clearing command
    using 'os.system()'.

    No arguments are required or processed.

    It does not delete files, erase command history, or undo your
    questionable life choices. It merely makes them harder to see.

EXAMPLES
    > clear
        Clears the terminal screen.

    > cls
        Does exactly the same thing.

        Windows users: finally, some representation.

    > clear
        Followed immediately by accidentally scrolling up.

        The terminal remembers.
        The terminal always remembers.

FUN FACTS
    - 'clear' uses 'clear' on Unix-like systems.
    - 'clear' uses 'cls' on Windows.
    - The 'cls' alias works regardless of which operating system
      you're actually running.
    - The command does not require any arguments.
    - It clears the screen, not your shell history.
    - It also cannot clear your browser history, your git history,
      or the memory of that production incident.

